        "thread_id": "TH1",
        "thread_goal": "Align on scope, constraints, and sequence for the pilot floor renovation.",
        "messages": [
          {
            "msg_id": "M1",
            "speaker_id": 100996,
            "reply_to": null,
            "text": "Team, let's kick off the Standardized Interior Renovation Pilot. The goal is to get this floor done efficiently and create a solid process for the next ones. First step is to align on the plan. I've put together a proposed sequence of tasks. What do you all think?",
            "mentions": [
              83191,
              262009,
              262702,
              334628
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M2",
            "speaker_id": 100996,
            "reply_to": "M1",
            "text": "My suggestion for the main workflow is: T1 Survey -> T2 Prep -> T3 Drywall -> T4 Plaster -> T5 Paint -> T6 Tiling. This should prevent us from having to redo work, like getting paint on newly laid tiles.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T1",
              "T2",
              "T3",
              "T4",
              "T5",
              "T6"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "👍",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M3",
            "speaker_id": 262702,
            "reply_to": "M2",
            "text": "That sequence makes sense. The key will be a thorough job on T2 surface prep before we even think about T4 plaster or T5 paint. A good foundation saves time later. We should use the A1 survey sheet to be really detailed in our inspection during T1.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A1"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "heavy_check_mark",
                "count": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M4",
            "speaker_id": 262009,
            "reply_to": "M2",
            "text": "The order looks good. Quick question on scope: which areas are we covering? Is it the entire floor, or just the main office space and the two new meeting rooms?",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M5",
            "speaker_id": 83191,
            "reply_to": "M2",
            "text": "So we do all the painting before any tiling? I thought maybe we tile the wet areas first so they can set.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M6",
            "speaker_id": 334628,
            "reply_to": "M2",
            "text": "ok sounds good",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "👍",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M7",
            "speaker_id": 100996,
            "reply_to": "M5",
            "text": "Good question. It's better to paint first. We can mask the floors, but it's much harder to protect new grout and tiles from paint splatter. We'll make sure the pantry and restroom floors are prepped (T2) and then come back for tiling (T6) after the main painting is done.",
            "mentions": [
              83191
